|segment[ver-silent-absence-reads-as-success] :type hypothesis :status discussion-grade :stage draft
  :max-attainable empirical
  :tags [verification tooling silence]
  :from .to-integrate/successor-session-quotes.md
  :from .to-integrate/agent-reports-2026-07-30.md

  |title A silent nothing is indistinguishable from a checked nothing

  |summary
    Where a failure, an empty result, and an unrun check all present as silence, the
    reader cannot tell them apart — and every one of them reads as success.

  |formal-expression
    A check that finds nothing and a check that never ran produce the same
    observable: no output. So does a check that crashed, a scope that matched
    nothing, and a claim of absence nobody made. In each case the *shape* of the
    result is a clean report, and a clean report is read as a pass.

    The asymmetry is what makes this worth stating as its own claim rather than as
    an instance of carelessness. A wrong answer is a claim, and a claim can collide
    with another claim — the mechanism the whole segment arrangement rests on. **An
    absent answer makes no claim, so it can collide with nothing**, and no amount of
    downstream discipline recovers it.

    Two consequences follow, and they point at different repairs. For **tools**:
    distinguish the states in the interface — an empty result, a failed run, and an
    unmatched scope should not share an exit code or an output stream. For
    **claims**: an absence claim must name the search that produced it, and the
    search must have run.

  |epistemic-status
    **Eight specimens, one evening, five of them found independently by three agents
    who were not looking for this:**

    1. A search tool printed the prose `No results found.` to stdout and exited `0`
    under `--json`, so a genuine empty result, a crashed query, and a scope matching
    nothing were one observable. 2. `--in` was `nargs='+'` and silently consumed the
    query as another scope pattern, returning zero hits with no error. 3.
    `--scope-debug` output resisted parsing because a delimiter was multibyte; `grep
    -o` returned nothing and the caller read that as a zero count. 4. A `grep -rl …
    | head` test always exited `0`, nearly turning an inconclusive check into a
    pass. 5. A stale-exemption loop failed silently under `set -e` on the healthy
    path. 6. An absence claim was written as searched when the search had not run;
    the thing it claimed absent was 78 seconds away in the same record. 7. A
    verifier's clean report was read as a pass by its author; the defects were
    outside its scope, and only a *missing expected item* in the output revealed it.
    8. A span carve that searched two primaries as one concatenated string matched a
    start in the first and an end in the second — producing a wrong, non-empty, 121
    KB result that nothing complained about.

    Held at **discussion-grade**: eight instances is a lot for one evening and none
    of it is measured. Every instance is from one estate, one night, and mostly one
    tool family, which is exactly the sampling that produces a spurious pattern.

    Max attainable: **empirical**, and the test is cheap — instrument a corpus of
    checks and searches, count how often a zero-result is produced by a fault rather
    than by the world, and compare against how often the caller distinguished them.
    Nobody has run it.

    Load-bearing: that the states are *observationally identical to the consumer*.
    If a reader could tell them apart with ordinary care, this reduces to
    carelessness and no structural repair is warranted.

  |discussion
    This is why an empty field in this corpus is required to *say* it is empty, why
    `bin/check-corpus` prints located-count and total rather than only failures, and
    why an unpaired finding must carry its search ( #ver-manufacture-originals, and
    the harvest brief's absence rule).

    It also explains something the corpus otherwise treats as coincidence: several
    of the day's most expensive errors were not wrong answers but *absent* ones — an
    unread commit body, an unopened original, fourteen messages that no extractor
    emitted. Those cost more than the wrong answers did, and took longer to find,
    because nothing contradicted them.

  |working-notes
    Regression guard: the natural repair — *be more careful with empty results* — is
    a disposition and loses ( #ver-act-not-intention). The repairs that worked were
    interface-level: distinguishable exit states, a count printed beside a pass, a
    required field that must be filled with the word *none*.

    Open: whether this is a distinct mechanism or the observable face of
    #ver-generation-is-self-ratifying — an absent result never presents as a moment
    either. Left unresolved deliberately; the interrelations across this corpus are
    not yet worth forcing.
